1
00:00:02,002 --> 00:00:03,179
GORDON: <i>Previously on</i> Gotham...

2
00:00:03,203 --> 00:00:04,762
You can't commit
a crime in Gotham

3
00:00:04,838 --> 00:00:06,830
<i>without a license from Penguin.</i>

4
00:00:06,940 --> 00:00:11,139
Tell your families Oswald
Cobblepot keeps them safe.

5
00:00:11,211 --> 00:00:13,612
Not the GCPD.

6
00:00:13,680 --> 00:00:15,876
HARVEY: <i>We'd need an
army to take on Penguin.</i>

7
00:00:15,983 --> 00:00:17,713
I am not giving this
city over to Penguin.

8
00:00:17,818 --> 00:00:19,229
<i>I don't care what the
Commissioner says.</i>

9
00:00:19,253 --> 00:00:22,155
- Makes me miss Falcone.
- I'm done with this business.

10
00:00:22,222 --> 00:00:23,349
I'm out.

11
00:00:23,423 --> 00:00:25,255
I've got a place down
south I can go to.

12
00:00:25,359 --> 00:00:27,692
We need an army. He's got one.

13
00:00:27,761 --> 00:00:30,629
HARVEY: <i>If you show your
face to Falcone, he will kill you.</i>

14
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:35,162
BARBARA: An
opportunity awaits you.

15
00:00:36,069 --> 00:00:37,503
Why do you want this so bad?

16
00:00:37,571 --> 00:00:40,405
I'm sick of not being
taken seriously.

17
00:00:40,507 --> 00:00:43,568
<i>With you and Barbara,
the three of us could give</i>

18
00:00:43,677 --> 00:00:45,771
all those creeps a
run for their money.

19
00:00:46,179 --> 00:00:48,614
You were the one time
I've met a weakened me.

20
00:00:48,715 --> 00:00:51,344
<i>I want you around
as a constant reminder</i>

21
00:00:51,418 --> 00:00:53,387
<i>to never make
that mistake again.</i>

22
00:00:54,621 --> 00:00:57,523
I'm the one you seek.
I am Ra's al Ghul.

23
00:00:58,358 --> 00:01:00,558
BRUCE: <i>I have to be ready
for when Ra's al Ghul returns.</i>

24
00:01:00,627 --> 00:01:02,892
ALFRED: <i>You need
to find your true north</i>

25
00:01:02,996 --> 00:01:03,996
<i>and let that guide you.</i>

26
00:01:16,443 --> 00:01:17,877
(HORSE SNORTS)

27
00:01:47,908 --> 00:01:49,501
It is here.

28
00:01:51,111 --> 00:01:52,238
At last.

29
00:02:24,344 --> 00:02:25,824
(SINISTER INSTRUMENTAL
MUSIC PLAYING)

30
00:02:26,113 --> 00:02:27,113
(GASPING)

31
00:02:33,487 --> 00:02:34,785
I was no more.

32
00:02:35,122 --> 00:02:36,283
Now, you live.

33
00:02:38,692 --> 00:02:39,692
How can this be?

34
00:02:40,193 --> 00:02:41,855
The power is in the water.

35
00:02:42,095 --> 00:02:43,529
It has given you life.

36
00:02:43,797 --> 00:02:45,095
And a destiny.

37
00:02:45,632 --> 00:02:47,157
You must find your heir.

38
00:02:48,035 --> 00:02:49,503
As I found you.

39
00:02:52,239 --> 00:02:53,537
Find him.

40
00:02:53,874 --> 00:02:55,069
Be free.

41
00:03:29,676 --> 00:03:30,676
Alfred? You read?

42
00:03:31,278 --> 00:03:33,713
Loud and clear, Master Bruce.

43
00:03:36,416 --> 00:03:38,027
Looks like the rumors
around town are true.

44
00:03:38,051 --> 00:03:39,679
Penguin's bringing
in a big shipment.

45
00:03:40,687 --> 00:03:42,007
Whatever it is,
must be valuable.

46
00:03:42,055 --> 00:03:44,047
He's got four armed
men loading it onto a truck.

47
00:03:45,058 --> 00:03:46,498
I'm gonna go down
for a closer look.

48
00:03:48,395 --> 00:03:50,489
How did I know you
were going to say that?

49
00:03:50,697 --> 00:03:52,598
I'm wearing bulletproof
armor, remember?

50
00:03:52,699 --> 00:03:55,464
Yes. Lucius Fox's
armor was bulletproof.

51
00:03:55,569 --> 00:03:57,470
My mask is not.

52
00:03:57,571 --> 00:04:00,097
Don't get shot in
the face? Got it.

53
00:04:00,307 --> 00:04:03,141
<i>Do I detect a note of
sarcasm, Master Bruce?</i>

54
00:04:03,376 --> 00:04:04,376
BRUCE: Hold on.

55
00:04:05,112 --> 00:04:07,072
I just picked up a thief
heading toward the truck.

56
00:04:07,414 --> 00:04:10,213
<i>Thieves stealing from thieves is
a victimless crime, Master Bruce.</i>

57
00:04:10,283 --> 00:04:11,546
<i>Just walk away now.</i>

58
00:04:12,919 --> 00:04:14,114
They're walking into a trap.

59
00:04:14,221 --> 00:04:16,690
Master Bruce, I'm all for
you protecting the innocent,

60
00:04:16,757 --> 00:04:19,386
but I won't have you
risking your life over a thief.

61
00:04:20,227 --> 00:04:21,559
<i>Now, walk away.</i>

62
00:04:23,997 --> 00:04:25,727
Master Bruce?

63
00:04:28,435 --> 00:04:29,903
<i>Master Bruce!</i>

64
00:05:11,111 --> 00:05:12,111
(GUN COCKING)

65
00:05:12,579 --> 00:05:14,741
STEVEDORE: The hell
are you supposed to be?

66
00:05:15,615 --> 00:05:18,175
You must be real stupid
trying to steal from Penguin.

67
00:05:22,322 --> 00:05:23,620
Bad idea.

68
00:05:29,462 --> 00:05:30,657
(GRUNTING)

69
00:05:35,135 --> 00:05:36,728
STEVEDORE 1: Hold it!

70
00:05:40,173 --> 00:05:41,937
(MEN GROANING)

71
00:05:42,542 --> 00:05:43,942
STEVEDORE 2:
Let's get outta here!

72
00:05:44,010 --> 00:05:45,103
Come on.

73
00:05:45,178 --> 00:05:46,441
In the truck.

74
00:05:46,513 --> 00:05:47,953
(ENGINE STARTS AND
TIRES SCREECHING)

75
00:05:51,051 --> 00:05:52,679
(SIGHS) Damn it!

76
00:05:53,620 --> 00:05:55,111
(BREATHES HEAVILY)

77
00:06:31,291 --> 00:06:32,418
(SPEAKS SPANISH)

78
00:06:33,193 --> 00:06:34,354
Can I help you?

79
00:06:35,428 --> 00:06:38,865
James Gordon. I'm here
to see Carmine Falcone.

80
00:06:40,233 --> 00:06:41,292
He's not expecting me.

81
00:06:43,770 --> 00:06:44,863
Come with me, please.

82
00:07:17,871 --> 00:07:19,066
Carmine.

83
00:07:21,574 --> 00:07:22,872
Jim.

84
00:07:24,044 --> 00:07:25,478
You're a long way from home.

85
00:07:27,480 --> 00:07:29,142
Came down to see you, Carmine.

86
00:07:30,784 --> 00:07:32,343
Heard you retired down here.

87
00:07:33,653 --> 00:07:35,178
Sorry for not calling ahead.

88
00:07:36,089 --> 00:07:37,523
No, you're not.

89
00:07:39,659 --> 00:07:41,127
It's quite the spread
you have here.

90
00:07:41,528 --> 00:07:43,861
I think we're past the
point of small talk, Jim.

91
00:07:44,130 --> 00:07:45,359
Just tell me why you're here.

92
00:07:50,570 --> 00:07:52,061
Morning, Daddy.

93
00:07:55,608 --> 00:07:56,667
FALCONE: How was Artemis?

94
00:07:56,776 --> 00:07:59,245
Feisty as usual. But I
whipped him into shape.

95
00:08:00,647 --> 00:08:01,706
Who's this?

96
00:08:02,182 --> 00:08:03,445
James Gordon.

97
00:08:05,485 --> 00:08:06,976
Jim. Meet my

98
00:08:07,520 --> 00:08:09,011
only daughter, Sofia.

99
00:08:18,999 --> 00:08:20,695
What're you doing here, James?

100
00:08:22,769 --> 00:08:24,032
FALCONE: Go ahead, Jim.

101
00:08:24,671 --> 00:08:26,264
I have no secrets
from my children.

102
00:08:28,875 --> 00:08:31,037
Penguin has taken over the city.

103
00:08:32,045 --> 00:08:34,844
GCPD has turned
its back on Gotham.

104
00:08:35,915 --> 00:08:37,884
There are families
that are still loyal to you.

105
00:08:38,551 --> 00:08:40,747
I need your help to
knock him off his perch.

106
00:08:41,488 --> 00:08:43,047
Sure this isn't about you, Jim?

107
00:08:44,024 --> 00:08:45,822
Your loss of power in Gotham?

108
00:08:46,092 --> 00:08:47,390
This is bigger than me.

109
00:08:48,194 --> 00:08:49,389
You have fight, kid.

110
00:08:49,562 --> 00:08:51,497
I've always admired that in you.

111
00:08:53,433 --> 00:08:55,265
But I can't do
what you're asking.

112
00:08:56,703 --> 00:08:57,898
Why?

113
00:09:01,541 --> 00:09:02,839
Because I'm dying.

114
00:09:04,077 --> 00:09:05,545
Doctors don't know why.

115
00:09:06,046 --> 00:09:07,810
Maybe old age. Karma.

116
00:09:09,682 --> 00:09:12,083
They seem to think the air
is better down here for me.

117
00:09:12,185 --> 00:09:13,312
Give me more time.

118
00:09:14,054 --> 00:09:16,080
I'm not one to
argue with doctors.

119
00:09:16,823 --> 00:09:18,348
Or time.

120
00:09:19,025 --> 00:09:20,516
So, you see. I can't
go back with you.

121
00:09:25,331 --> 00:09:26,390
I'll go back.

122
00:09:26,866 --> 00:09:27,866
Hush.

123
00:09:28,902 --> 00:09:30,734
You're not ready for Gotham.

124
00:09:33,406 --> 00:09:34,704
There was a time in my life

125
00:09:34,774 --> 00:09:36,606
when I would have
done anything for the city.

126
00:09:37,944 --> 00:09:39,310
Those days are gone.

127
00:09:41,081 --> 00:09:42,447
I'm sorry, Jim.

128
00:09:43,116 --> 00:09:44,379
You're on your own.

129
00:09:53,293 --> 00:09:55,057
You sure you know
what you're doing?

130
00:09:56,796 --> 00:09:57,957
(WINCES)

131
00:09:58,731 --> 00:10:00,691
You're mad because I
didn't listen to you, Alfred.

132
00:10:00,733 --> 00:10:02,929
You're damn bloody
right I am. Yeah.

133
00:10:07,307 --> 00:10:09,606
You're right. I let
my guard down.

134
00:10:10,710 --> 00:10:11,973
It won't happen again.

135
00:10:13,947 --> 00:10:15,279
All right. There we go.

136
00:10:19,886 --> 00:10:21,548
Might leave a
little bit of a scar.

137
00:10:27,093 --> 00:10:28,254
Thanks, Alfred.

138
00:10:28,328 --> 00:10:29,626
All right.

139
00:10:33,399 --> 00:10:35,439
(STUTTERING) Now... Where
do you think you're going?

140
00:10:35,568 --> 00:10:36,627
To the docks.

141
00:10:37,003 --> 00:10:38,972
I'm sure there's a
manifest on the freighter.

142
00:10:39,038 --> 00:10:40,683
Whatever is in that
crate must be important

143
00:10:40,707 --> 00:10:42,767
if the thief was willing
to steal from the Penguin.

144
00:10:42,842 --> 00:10:43,882
I want to know what it is.

145
00:10:43,943 --> 00:10:45,321
Right, so being shot
wasn't enough, was it?

146
00:10:45,345 --> 00:10:47,473
You gotta let this
slide, Master B.

147
00:10:47,714 --> 00:10:50,309
There's more to this,
Alfred. I can feel it.

148
00:10:50,950 --> 00:10:53,317
This is my chance to
investigate a real crime.

149
00:10:53,987 --> 00:10:55,717
Stop it before it becomes
something bigger.

150
00:11:00,360 --> 00:11:01,521
Fine.

151
00:11:01,861 --> 00:11:04,607
But just don't go charging around in
broad daylight with that bloody mask on.

152
00:11:04,631 --> 00:11:07,431
It'll be like having a socking great
bulls-eye on your back, all right?

153
00:11:07,567 --> 00:11:09,263
So how do you suggest
I get onto the ship?

154
00:11:15,008 --> 00:11:17,273
There are other kinds
of masks you can wear,

155
00:11:17,677 --> 00:11:18,975
Master Bruce.

156
00:11:22,482 --> 00:11:23,482
(SHUDDERING)

157
00:11:25,451 --> 00:11:26,451
(GRUNTS)

158
00:11:27,954 --> 00:11:28,954
(GUN COCKING)

159
00:11:29,055 --> 00:11:32,890
As you can see, the scatter
pattern is tighter than most.

160
00:11:33,393 --> 00:11:36,056
You won't find a superior
weapon anywhere in Gotham.

161
00:11:36,563 --> 00:11:38,998
- I'll take two dozen.
- Excellent.

162
00:11:39,199 --> 00:11:40,997
You can pay out front.

163
00:11:43,670 --> 00:11:45,195
MAN: Right this way, sir.

164
00:11:45,438 --> 00:11:46,872
Thank you, everyone, for coming.

165
00:11:47,207 --> 00:11:49,108
Tabitha planning
on coming to work?

166
00:11:49,676 --> 00:11:51,440
I am paying her
half the profits.

167
00:11:51,611 --> 00:11:53,512
Don't you mean a third?

168
00:11:53,580 --> 00:11:54,946
That depends.

169
00:11:55,381 --> 00:11:56,576
Where's my crate?

170
00:11:58,084 --> 00:11:59,575
I ran into some trouble.

171
00:11:59,953 --> 00:12:01,581
- Did Penguin's men see you?
- No.

172
00:12:01,854 --> 00:12:03,846
I wore a mask like you said.

173
00:12:04,023 --> 00:12:06,083
- I know you're pissed...
- I'm not mad.

174
00:12:06,593 --> 00:12:08,061
I misjudged you.

175
00:12:09,529 --> 00:12:11,589
I was wrong to cut you
in on my new business.

176
00:12:12,599 --> 00:12:14,033
You're clearly not ready.

177
00:12:16,069 --> 00:12:17,332
You're serious?

178
00:12:17,403 --> 00:12:18,928
I can get the damn thing.

179
00:12:19,038 --> 00:12:20,563
I'll do it myself.

180
00:12:23,243 --> 00:12:25,178
But if you do want
to redeem yourself,

181
00:12:25,245 --> 00:12:27,510
you can do the floors.
Mop's in the closet.

182
00:12:27,580 --> 00:12:28,580
(SCOFFS)

183
00:12:29,916 --> 00:12:32,215
You can mop your
own damn floors.

184
00:12:35,855 --> 00:12:38,051
PENGUIN: Another excellent
night of business, Victor.

185
00:12:38,458 --> 00:12:39,551
Except for the part where

186
00:12:39,626 --> 00:12:41,595
a couple of thieves tried
to rob your shipment.

187
00:12:41,694 --> 00:12:42,718
What?

188
00:12:42,795 --> 00:12:44,161
Relax, boss.

189
00:12:44,264 --> 00:12:47,098
My men stopped them before they
could get their hands on anything.

190
00:12:48,167 --> 00:12:49,635
You see what I'm
dealing with, Ed?

191
00:12:52,472 --> 00:12:55,067
That shipment is full of black
market items I'm auctioning off

192
00:12:55,141 --> 00:12:57,076
tonight to Gotham's elite.

193
00:12:57,610 --> 00:12:58,908
I stand to make a killing.

194
00:12:59,846 --> 00:13:02,179
I cannot do that
without the items.

195
00:13:02,282 --> 00:13:03,511
Where are they?

196
00:13:03,616 --> 00:13:05,141
At the warehouse.

197
00:13:05,251 --> 00:13:06,275
Take me.

198
00:13:07,420 --> 00:13:09,753
I want to be sure
it's all accounted for.

199
00:13:10,323 --> 00:13:12,952
Once I do, bring everything
back here for safekeeping.

200
00:13:19,499 --> 00:13:20,499
(DOOR OPENING)

201
00:13:23,069 --> 00:13:24,069
(DOOR CLOSES)

202
00:13:33,446 --> 00:13:34,846
(BREATHES HEAVILY)

203
00:13:39,018 --> 00:13:40,018
(ICE CRACKING)

204
00:13:51,964 --> 00:13:54,229
No! No!

205
00:13:55,568 --> 00:13:58,128
No!

206
00:13:58,471 --> 00:13:59,734
(GASPS)

207
00:13:59,839 --> 00:14:01,831
This cannot be happening.

208
00:14:01,908 --> 00:14:05,037
I don't know. Kind
of opens up the room.

209
00:14:07,380 --> 00:14:08,848
Whoever did this,

210
00:14:08,915 --> 00:14:10,315
I want them found.

211
00:14:10,750 --> 00:14:12,309
I want them dead.

212
00:14:12,685 --> 00:14:15,177
And I want Ed back here, today!

213
00:14:19,359 --> 00:14:20,359
(DOOR OPENS)

214
00:14:22,829 --> 00:14:23,829
(BREATHES HEAVILY)

215
00:14:27,900 --> 00:14:29,300
Where are you, Ed?

216
00:14:36,309 --> 00:14:37,309
(WOMAN GASPS)

217
00:14:38,544 --> 00:14:39,637
Oh! He's up. He's up.

218
00:14:39,712 --> 00:14:42,409
Where am I? How did I get here?

219
00:14:42,515 --> 00:14:45,246
WOMAN: I brought you here.
You're in my home. What do you think?

220
00:14:45,718 --> 00:14:46,718
(NYGMA GASPING)

221
00:14:50,590 --> 00:14:53,560
I'm sorry, but who are you?

222
00:14:54,293 --> 00:14:56,728
You don't remember?
Hilltop Elementary?

223
00:14:57,196 --> 00:14:58,630
Mrs. Macher's class?

224
00:14:59,031 --> 00:15:01,523
I was third row,
closest to the window?

225
00:15:02,135 --> 00:15:04,400
Eddie. It's me. Myrtle Jenkins.

226
00:15:04,704 --> 00:15:05,704
(LAUGHS)

227
00:15:06,272 --> 00:15:08,537
Um, I followed your rise
to becoming the Riddler.

228
00:15:08,941 --> 00:15:10,569
I've read every article.

229
00:15:10,643 --> 00:15:12,077
All the police reports.

230
00:15:13,045 --> 00:15:15,105
I guess you could say
I'm your number-one fan.

231
00:15:15,748 --> 00:15:16,875
Okay.

232
00:15:18,217 --> 00:15:21,119
Um, I should get going.

233
00:15:21,254 --> 00:15:22,722
Whoop! That's gonna be tough.

234
00:15:23,756 --> 00:15:24,756
(GASPS)

235
00:15:25,191 --> 00:15:26,921
(BREATHES HEAVILY)

236
00:15:28,861 --> 00:15:30,193
Why am I tied up?

237
00:15:30,863 --> 00:15:32,092
MYRTLE: You're not.

238
00:15:32,532 --> 00:15:35,468
Your muscles have atrophied
from being on ice. Okay?

239
00:15:35,568 --> 00:15:38,436
But don't worry, these warming
lamps should help thaw you out.

240
00:15:39,238 --> 00:15:41,070
- Ice?
- Yeah.

241
00:15:42,108 --> 00:15:45,374
Last thing I remember was
being frozen by the river.

242
00:15:47,547 --> 00:15:49,448
How long was I on ice?

243
00:15:51,751 --> 00:15:52,751
Five months.

244
00:15:52,919 --> 00:15:53,919
(GASPS)

245
00:15:54,454 --> 00:15:55,454
I know.

246
00:15:55,521 --> 00:15:56,955
Since then,

247
00:15:57,323 --> 00:16:00,122
Penguin opened a club
called the Iceberg Lounge.

248
00:16:01,461 --> 00:16:03,089
You were its centerpiece.

249
00:16:03,696 --> 00:16:05,130
NYGMA: That little freak.

250
00:16:05,631 --> 00:16:08,624
He put me on display
like some caged animal.

251
00:16:09,001 --> 00:16:10,025
Yes.

252
00:16:10,136 --> 00:16:13,629
And I will nurse you back to health
so you can reclaim your throne

253
00:16:13,840 --> 00:16:16,537
as Gotham's greatest villain.

254
00:16:17,376 --> 00:16:19,504
Ha! Ooh! Caveat.

255
00:16:19,612 --> 00:16:21,342
With me as your
sidekick, of course.

256
00:16:22,682 --> 00:16:24,981
Doesn't that sound
wonderful, my love?

257
00:16:27,653 --> 00:16:28,653
(EXHALES)

258
00:16:38,097 --> 00:16:39,156
You lost, kid?

259
00:16:39,966 --> 00:16:41,764
My uncle Al works on your ship.

260
00:16:42,435 --> 00:16:44,927
My aunt hasn't heard from him
in a few days, and she's worried.

261
00:16:45,538 --> 00:16:47,268
There're a few men below deck.

262
00:16:47,340 --> 00:16:50,333
But I'm pretty damn sure none of
their old ladies are looking for 'em.

263
00:16:50,710 --> 00:16:52,736
Listen, I told my
aunt I'd find him.

264
00:16:53,346 --> 00:16:56,009
Can I just take a quick look? I'll
be in and out in a few minutes,

265
00:16:56,082 --> 00:16:57,082
I promise.

266
00:16:58,918 --> 00:17:00,716
Sure. Go ahead.

267
00:17:02,321 --> 00:17:03,321
Thank you.

268
00:17:20,273 --> 00:17:21,273
(DOOR CLOSES)

269
00:17:47,266 --> 00:17:48,266
(FOOTSTEPS APPROACHING)

270
00:17:51,170 --> 00:17:53,196
Bet you think you're real
smart giving us the slip.

271
00:17:55,274 --> 00:17:56,902
I don't know what
you're talking about...

272
00:17:56,976 --> 00:17:59,741
Save it. Somebody tried to
steal from this ship last night.

273
00:18:00,079 --> 00:18:01,707
Did this to my face.

274
00:18:02,214 --> 00:18:03,910
Tell me who you're working for.

275
00:18:03,983 --> 00:18:06,782
I assure you, I'm just
here looking for my uncle.

276
00:18:06,886 --> 00:18:08,320
I took a wrong turn.

277
00:18:08,421 --> 00:18:09,719
Assure?

278
00:18:09,789 --> 00:18:11,121
Here's a little advice.

279
00:18:11,223 --> 00:18:13,317
If you're gonna play
the part of a street kid,

280
00:18:13,426 --> 00:18:14,985
learn how to talk like one.

281
00:18:15,094 --> 00:18:16,756
Hey, Andy.

282
00:18:17,096 --> 00:18:18,155
Get over here.

283
00:18:18,965 --> 00:18:21,298
What'd I tell you about
bothering me at work?

284
00:18:23,235 --> 00:18:24,259
Sorry, fellas.

285
00:18:28,240 --> 00:18:29,401
You're right, though.

286
00:18:29,809 --> 00:18:32,108
The boy's acting does
need some work, doesn't it?

287
00:18:32,378 --> 00:18:33,378
(GRUNTING)

288
00:18:37,483 --> 00:18:40,942
You see, improvisation, Master
Bruce, is a very important weapon

289
00:18:41,053 --> 00:18:42,419
in any actor's toolkit.

290
00:18:43,823 --> 00:18:44,823
Shall we go?

291
00:18:50,463 --> 00:18:53,092
There's a knife being
sold at your auction tonight.

292
00:18:53,633 --> 00:18:55,431
I was thinking you
might be kind enough

293
00:18:55,501 --> 00:18:57,299
to sell it to me ahead of time.

294
00:19:00,973 --> 00:19:02,669
And why is this knife
so important to you?

295
00:19:03,809 --> 00:19:05,107
It's not.

296
00:19:05,177 --> 00:19:06,645
I have a client
who's interested.

297
00:19:07,513 --> 00:19:08,537
Gave me a ton of cash.

298
00:19:08,648 --> 00:19:11,311
So, I was hoping to get it for a
lower price and make a profit.

299
00:19:12,451 --> 00:19:15,615
That start with the attempted
five-finger discount last night?

300
00:19:16,022 --> 00:19:17,650
I have no idea what
you're talking about.

301
00:19:18,624 --> 00:19:19,683
Of course you don't.

302
00:19:20,693 --> 00:19:22,491
I'll triple the opening price.

303
00:19:22,561 --> 00:19:24,223
It'll probably go
for less at auction.

304
00:19:24,330 --> 00:19:26,356
(CHUCKLES) My dear,

305
00:19:26,699 --> 00:19:29,328
wherever you disappeared
to these last few months

306
00:19:29,435 --> 00:19:31,666
has not made you a better liar.

307
00:19:33,639 --> 00:19:36,108
It's clear that this
isn't about profit,

308
00:19:36,642 --> 00:19:39,009
but about your
mysterious benefactor.

309
00:19:39,679 --> 00:19:41,614
He is your client,
(STUTTERS) is he not?

310
00:19:48,454 --> 00:19:49,854
Reveal his identity to me

311
00:19:49,955 --> 00:19:51,321
and you have a deal.

312
00:19:56,495 --> 00:19:59,329
Wow. He's really
got his hooks into you.

313
00:20:01,767 --> 00:20:03,497
See you tonight, I guess.

314
00:20:03,569 --> 00:20:05,231
Don't forget to bring
your checkbook.

315
00:20:13,913 --> 00:20:15,677
Always a pleasure, Oswald.

316
00:20:41,941 --> 00:20:44,035
BRUCE: The knife was
used to embalm King Balahsi.

317
00:20:44,443 --> 00:20:47,204
According to this, he ruled ancient
Mesopotamia for the first century.

318
00:20:48,214 --> 00:20:50,877
What thief in Gotham
would want a knife like that?

319
00:20:51,117 --> 00:20:52,585
That's a good question.

320
00:20:52,852 --> 00:20:55,253
According to the manifest,
the shipment was full of jewels

321
00:20:55,354 --> 00:20:57,448
and expensive art. Yet,
our thief went for the item

322
00:20:57,556 --> 00:20:58,888
with the least intrinsic value.

323
00:20:59,125 --> 00:21:01,492
Well, that tells me
she was a gun for hire.

324
00:21:02,561 --> 00:21:03,790
But who hired her?

325
00:21:10,469 --> 00:21:11,960
I think I just found the answer.

326
00:21:12,571 --> 00:21:13,766
Alfred, look.

327
00:21:14,406 --> 00:21:17,740
This drawing depicts a
man being healed by water.

328
00:21:18,444 --> 00:21:19,673
And look at his eyes.

329
00:21:21,580 --> 00:21:22,639
His face.

330
00:21:26,118 --> 00:21:27,484
Who does that remind you of?

331
00:21:30,322 --> 00:21:31,517
That's impossible.

332
00:21:31,624 --> 00:21:34,423
I mean, this drawing
is 2,000 years old.

333
00:21:34,493 --> 00:21:35,722
I know.

334
00:21:42,067 --> 00:21:43,399
That's him.

335
00:21:44,770 --> 00:21:47,137
That's Ra's al Ghul.

336
00:21:50,442 --> 00:21:51,842
He wants the knife.

337
00:21:59,585 --> 00:22:01,110
I struck out with Falcone.

338
00:22:01,787 --> 00:22:02,811
HARVEY: <i>That right?</i>

339
00:22:04,990 --> 00:22:06,652
Try not to sound
too happy about it.

340
00:22:06,725 --> 00:22:08,523
<i>Look, if you brought
back Falcone,</i>

341
00:22:08,627 --> 00:22:10,892
<i>that would mean gang
wars and blood in the streets.</i>

342
00:22:10,996 --> 00:22:13,158
<i>And I don't like it when
the streets are bloody, Jim.</i>

343
00:22:13,232 --> 00:22:15,394
Yeah, see how you
like it with Penguin.

344
00:22:16,535 --> 00:22:17,662
See you tomorrow, Harvey.

345
00:22:20,906 --> 00:22:22,033
SOFIA: Mind if I join you?

346
00:22:29,348 --> 00:22:30,680
What are you doing here?

347
00:22:31,317 --> 00:22:34,219
I figured you didn't know anyone in
town. Thought I'd keep you company.

348
00:22:34,553 --> 00:22:37,318
Or maybe I just didn't
want to drink alone.

349
00:22:42,228 --> 00:22:43,389
To my dad.

350
00:22:51,503 --> 00:22:53,028
I am sorry about your father.

351
00:22:54,673 --> 00:22:56,151
We may have had our
differences over the years,

352
00:22:56,175 --> 00:22:57,302
but I always respected him.

353
00:22:59,378 --> 00:23:00,378
(CHUCKLES SOFTLY)

354
00:23:01,547 --> 00:23:03,038
When I was a little girl,

355
00:23:03,582 --> 00:23:04,960
if I wanted to spend
time with my father,

356
00:23:04,984 --> 00:23:06,953
he'd have me schedule
an appointment.

357
00:23:07,720 --> 00:23:09,951
Save the respect. I
know who my father is.

358
00:23:11,357 --> 00:23:13,952
Took him getting sick for us to
finally have a real relationship.

359
00:23:21,367 --> 00:23:22,733
How long have you
lived down here?

360
00:23:24,136 --> 00:23:25,798
Since I was 13.

361
00:23:26,105 --> 00:23:28,700
My father thought sending me away
from Gotham would keep me safe.

362
00:23:30,075 --> 00:23:32,203
I mean, there are
worse places to grow up.

363
00:23:33,279 --> 00:23:34,645
People are happy here.

364
00:23:34,747 --> 00:23:36,545
You see the sun every day.

365
00:23:36,615 --> 00:23:38,584
And I wasn't alone. I had Mario.

366
00:23:40,019 --> 00:23:41,544
We took care of each other.

367
00:23:47,293 --> 00:23:49,057
I know there's
nothing I can say.

368
00:23:51,263 --> 00:23:53,027
I am sorry for what
happened to him.

369
00:23:58,904 --> 00:24:01,635
You're right. There is
nothing you can say.

370
00:24:03,542 --> 00:24:05,033
He was infected with the virus.

371
00:24:05,744 --> 00:24:07,906
You did what you had to.

372
00:24:16,488 --> 00:24:17,488
(MUSIC PLAYING)

373
00:24:18,123 --> 00:24:20,752
There's no sign of Nygma
anywhere in Gotham.

374
00:24:21,860 --> 00:24:23,351
Word on the street is

375
00:24:23,829 --> 00:24:25,422
it's an inside job.

376
00:24:26,465 --> 00:24:28,434
Find whoever is
in charge of hiring.

377
00:24:28,500 --> 00:24:29,627
Kill them.

378
00:24:29,835 --> 00:24:31,269
I want everyone
here interrogated.

379
00:24:32,271 --> 00:24:33,534
There's a traitor amongst us.

380
00:24:40,412 --> 00:24:41,412
(GASPS)

381
00:24:44,116 --> 00:24:46,984
MYRTLE: Those needles should
help stimulate your muscles.

382
00:24:47,252 --> 00:24:49,016
You'll have feeling
back in no time.

383
00:24:49,254 --> 00:24:51,519
(LAUGHING)

384
00:24:51,623 --> 00:24:53,251
Just call me the Riddlette!

385
00:24:53,826 --> 00:24:55,795
I know. What do you think?

386
00:24:55,861 --> 00:24:59,298
I think that rehashing someone
else's idea isn't very original.

387
00:25:00,966 --> 00:25:01,966
(SIGHS)

388
00:25:02,034 --> 00:25:03,502
It's an homage.

389
00:25:03,602 --> 00:25:04,968
And I thought you'd like it.

390
00:25:10,175 --> 00:25:11,666
It's very nice.

391
00:25:13,078 --> 00:25:14,569
(GIGGLES)

392
00:25:16,415 --> 00:25:18,008
This is so much fun.

393
00:25:18,517 --> 00:25:20,679
If only I were the criminal
mastermind you are,

394
00:25:20,753 --> 00:25:22,381
we would've been
together sooner.

395
00:25:23,355 --> 00:25:24,914
I'm sorry, I don't
really follow that.

396
00:25:25,557 --> 00:25:27,492
Well, it took me months
to plan your escape.

397
00:25:27,760 --> 00:25:30,594
I had to learn Penguin's
schedule. When he came and went.

398
00:25:31,063 --> 00:25:32,656
Devise an exit strategy.

399
00:25:33,399 --> 00:25:35,994
I'm sure it would've taken you
two seconds to think something up.

400
00:25:37,770 --> 00:25:40,501
It is slightly impressive you
were able to outwit Oswald.

401
00:25:40,706 --> 00:25:41,706
MYRTLE: Hmm.

402
00:25:42,074 --> 00:25:44,475
Though, as you say, my plan
would've taken much less time.

403
00:25:44,543 --> 00:25:46,068
Oh, wait. That is a great idea.

404
00:25:46,178 --> 00:25:48,079
You should tell me what
your plan would've been.

405
00:25:48,180 --> 00:25:51,241
Because a window into your master
villainy would mean so much to me.

406
00:26:08,333 --> 00:26:09,333
Um...

407
00:26:09,401 --> 00:26:11,097
(BREATHES HEAVILY)

408
00:26:14,206 --> 00:26:15,206
Oh.

409
00:26:15,274 --> 00:26:17,074
Here I am bombarding
you with silly questions.

410
00:26:17,109 --> 00:26:19,874
After everything you've been
through, you must be exhausted.

411
00:26:20,212 --> 00:26:21,212
Yes.

412
00:26:22,247 --> 00:26:24,307
- Yes, I must be exhausted.
- (BELL DINGS)

413
00:26:24,416 --> 00:26:25,475
Ooh. Soup's ready.

414
00:26:25,918 --> 00:26:28,888
Do you like tomato? I put
little question marks in it, too.

415
00:26:50,476 --> 00:26:51,705
Last one.

416
00:26:52,611 --> 00:26:54,239
One. Two. Three.

417
00:26:59,751 --> 00:27:01,083
Anything, my love?

418
00:27:06,425 --> 00:27:08,985
No! Acupuncture

419
00:27:10,095 --> 00:27:11,757
is therapeutic snake oil.

420
00:27:13,499 --> 00:27:15,968
I've been focused on
repairing the wrong muscle.

421
00:27:16,034 --> 00:27:17,127
What?

422
00:27:17,202 --> 00:27:18,693
Your brain.

423
00:27:18,804 --> 00:27:21,672
It fires synapses that carry
neurons to the rest of your body.

424
00:27:21,773 --> 00:27:23,173
If we can stimulate it,

425
00:27:23,275 --> 00:27:25,005
perhaps your other
muscles will follow.

426
00:27:25,878 --> 00:27:27,039
- Logical.
- Yeah.

427
00:27:27,713 --> 00:27:28,873
- What do you suggest?
- Well,

428
00:27:29,615 --> 00:27:32,949
in anticipation of our partnership,
I have been working on a few

429
00:27:33,185 --> 00:27:34,517
riddles.

430
00:27:34,620 --> 00:27:35,815
Yes!

431
00:27:36,355 --> 00:27:39,689
Riddles! That's
exactly what I need.

432
00:27:40,859 --> 00:27:42,623
That's gonna make me
feel like myself again.

433
00:27:43,795 --> 00:27:44,854
Fire away.

434
00:27:44,963 --> 00:27:46,295
Okay. Here goes.

435
00:27:46,598 --> 00:27:48,829
What's black and
white and red all over?

436
00:27:49,168 --> 00:27:52,696
Easy. Zebra with
a gaping hole. Next.

437
00:27:53,272 --> 00:27:54,706
No, it's actually a newspaper.

438
00:27:55,541 --> 00:27:58,375
That's ridiculous. There's
nothing red on a newspaper. Next.

439
00:27:58,710 --> 00:28:00,542
- Well, it makes sense, if...
- Next!

440
00:28:00,646 --> 00:28:01,646
Um...

441
00:28:02,181 --> 00:28:05,276
I have a face and two
hands, but no arms or legs.

442
00:28:05,651 --> 00:28:06,812
What am I?

443
00:28:07,019 --> 00:28:08,783
Deformed baby. Come on.

444
00:28:08,987 --> 00:28:10,455
Surely, you can
do better than this.

445
00:28:10,722 --> 00:28:12,884
The answer is a clock. Hands...

446
00:28:16,495 --> 00:28:19,090
I think something is
wrong with your brain, Ed.

447
00:28:20,499 --> 00:28:21,933
There is

448
00:28:22,701 --> 00:28:24,932
nothing wrong with my brain.

449
00:28:25,704 --> 00:28:27,696
The problem is your riddles.

450
00:28:28,740 --> 00:28:30,504
Bring me a riddle

451
00:28:30,709 --> 00:28:32,177
that is worthy of my name.

452
00:28:41,420 --> 00:28:43,340
GORDON: Next time, I'll
know to pack a swim suit.

453
00:28:44,022 --> 00:28:45,081
You actually own one?

454
00:28:46,225 --> 00:28:48,023
Yeah. I got a nice two-piece.

455
00:28:49,394 --> 00:28:51,329
He comes with a sense of humor.

456
00:28:56,201 --> 00:28:58,033
You ever think about
walking away from it all?

457
00:28:59,104 --> 00:29:00,231
Gotham?

458
00:29:02,174 --> 00:29:03,369
Sometimes.

459
00:29:04,676 --> 00:29:05,837
But I can't.

460
00:29:06,778 --> 00:29:08,041
Why not?

461
00:29:08,747 --> 00:29:09,942
It's in my blood.

462
00:29:11,383 --> 00:29:14,046
My father spent his entire
life fighting for the city.

463
00:29:14,519 --> 00:29:15,851
Never took the easy way out.

464
00:29:16,688 --> 00:29:18,054
Neither can I.

465
00:29:23,395 --> 00:29:24,395
What?

466
00:29:26,431 --> 00:29:27,763
Give 'em to me.

467
00:29:30,669 --> 00:29:32,262
I don't really think
they're your size.

468
00:29:33,105 --> 00:29:34,129
Come on.

469
00:29:35,807 --> 00:29:37,105
- That too?
- Mmm-hmm.

470
00:29:37,609 --> 00:29:39,771
- What are you doing?
- Come on.

471
00:29:42,347 --> 00:29:43,679
You can't be serious.

472
00:29:45,517 --> 00:29:46,985
Just a little water, Jim.

473
00:30:09,775 --> 00:30:12,108
GORDON: I can't remember
the last time I just stood still.

474
00:30:45,043 --> 00:30:46,375
All right, Master Bruce.

475
00:30:47,512 --> 00:30:48,844
Let's go over the plan again.

476
00:30:49,648 --> 00:30:50,741
It's simple.

477
00:30:51,350 --> 00:30:53,182
I show up at the
auction and buy the knife.

478
00:30:53,552 --> 00:30:55,544
Should be easy
enough. I am a billionaire.

479
00:30:55,654 --> 00:30:57,680
And a very public one at that.

480
00:30:58,490 --> 00:31:00,686
Turning up at the Penguin's
black market auction

481
00:31:00,792 --> 00:31:03,159
is certainly gonna raise
a few eyebrows. Innit?

482
00:31:04,896 --> 00:31:07,456
You're the one that said that
bidding on it was the smarter play.

483
00:31:07,899 --> 00:31:10,733
That I did. But I didn't mean
going in as yourself. Did I?

484
00:31:11,403 --> 00:31:12,632
What do you think the idea was

485
00:31:12,704 --> 00:31:15,333
going down to the docks
pretending to be someone else?

486
00:31:15,540 --> 00:31:18,032
I was trying to
teach you a lesson.

487
00:31:18,710 --> 00:31:21,578
Now, if you're going to
continue down this vigilante path,

488
00:31:21,680 --> 00:31:22,841
Master Bruce,

489
00:31:23,715 --> 00:31:25,547
you've got to learn
how to assume

490
00:31:26,618 --> 00:31:28,052
other roles.

491
00:31:37,596 --> 00:31:38,859
Play the role

492
00:31:39,598 --> 00:31:40,896
that you were born to play.

493
00:31:45,804 --> 00:31:47,466
That of a privileged someone.

494
00:31:48,473 --> 00:31:50,567
Pedantic teenager
with deeper pockets

495
00:31:50,642 --> 00:31:52,235
than anybody else in this city.

496
00:31:54,246 --> 00:31:55,737
You mean behave
like a spoiled brat.

497
00:31:56,214 --> 00:31:57,443
It goes against everything

498
00:31:57,549 --> 00:31:59,950
that your beautiful parents
tried to ingrain in you.

499
00:32:01,386 --> 00:32:02,615
But, yes,

500
00:32:03,054 --> 00:32:04,113
sir.

501
00:32:08,927 --> 00:32:10,259
Bruce Wayne.

502
00:32:12,864 --> 00:32:14,526
Billionaire brat.

503
00:32:15,367 --> 00:32:17,029
Two hundred and fifty thousand.

504
00:32:17,369 --> 00:32:20,430
Two hundred and fifty thousand
dollars going once, twice...

505
00:32:21,106 --> 00:32:23,132
Sold. To Mr. Bruce Wayne.

506
00:32:23,542 --> 00:32:26,137
(LAUGHS) That's what
I'm talking about! I love art.

507
00:32:27,179 --> 00:32:28,738
I love it! Yeah!

508
00:32:29,080 --> 00:32:31,640
I'm gonna put it in my bathroom.

509
00:32:31,783 --> 00:32:32,783
(CROWD GASPS)

510
00:32:33,618 --> 00:32:36,349
Steady on, Master Bruce, let's
not overplay our hand, shall we?

511
00:32:36,455 --> 00:32:37,684
I must admit, this is fun.

512
00:32:37,789 --> 00:32:40,782
And try to exercise a
modicum of financial restraint.

513
00:32:40,859 --> 00:32:42,503
I know technically you are
the richest man in the room,

514
00:32:42,527 --> 00:32:45,258
but let's not try and buy
absolutely everything, shall we?

515
00:32:45,530 --> 00:32:47,809
True. But if I only bid on the
knife everyone's been trying to steal

516
00:32:47,833 --> 00:32:49,665
from Penguin, it might
draw his suspicion.

517
00:32:50,302 --> 00:32:53,204
If I go on a spending spree,
recklessly throwing my money around,

518
00:32:53,305 --> 00:32:54,305
he won't think twice.

519
00:32:54,506 --> 00:32:56,668
Nice. When did
you think of that?

520
00:32:57,042 --> 00:32:58,169
Improvisation, Alfred.

521
00:32:58,977 --> 00:33:00,969
It's a weapon in
any actor's toolkit.

522
00:33:01,680 --> 00:33:03,444
All right, sir. Here we go.

523
00:33:07,018 --> 00:33:09,112
Oswald! My friend.

524
00:33:09,788 --> 00:33:12,121
Bruce Wayne? What
a lovely surprise.

525
00:33:12,491 --> 00:33:15,154
You certainly are getting out
quite a lot these days. Aren't you?

526
00:33:15,327 --> 00:33:17,990
The way I see it, everyone
else in this town is having fun.

527
00:33:18,096 --> 00:33:20,122
- Why shouldn't I?
- I agree.

528
00:33:20,432 --> 00:33:22,458
You and your insanely
large bank account

529
00:33:22,534 --> 00:33:23,866
are more than welcome here.

530
00:33:24,102 --> 00:33:25,102
- Ha!
- (PENGUIN CHUCKLES)

531
00:33:25,971 --> 00:33:27,200
Barbara Kean.

532
00:33:28,340 --> 00:33:29,865
I thought she died.

533
00:33:30,842 --> 00:33:33,073
Unfortunately, nobody
stays dead in this town.

534
00:33:34,679 --> 00:33:35,977
Excuse me.

535
00:33:37,048 --> 00:33:38,641
You do realize, don't
you, Master Bruce,

536
00:33:38,717 --> 00:33:40,083
that if you actually win,

537
00:33:40,652 --> 00:33:42,348
Ra's al Ghul will
come after the knife.

538
00:33:45,423 --> 00:33:46,686
Let him.

539
00:33:46,758 --> 00:33:50,388
The next item up for
auction is an embalming knife.

540
00:33:50,896 --> 00:33:54,162
From the tomb of King
Balahsi of Sumeria.

541
00:33:55,033 --> 00:33:57,400
Opening bid will
begin at $50,000.

542
00:33:58,236 --> 00:34:00,728
A hundred thousand dollars.

543
00:34:01,306 --> 00:34:02,386
What does she want with it?

544
00:34:02,541 --> 00:34:04,169
I have no idea.

545
00:34:04,509 --> 00:34:05,670
Stay focused.

546
00:34:05,744 --> 00:34:07,508
One hundred thousand
dollars to Ms. Kean.

547
00:34:07,712 --> 00:34:11,479
- Do I hear...
- One hundred thousand and one dollar.

548
00:34:11,816 --> 00:34:14,547
AUCTIONEER: Actually,
Mr. Wayne, the bidding amounts are...

549
00:34:14,653 --> 00:34:16,815
- Two hundred thousand dollars.
- Fine.

550
00:34:16,888 --> 00:34:18,356
We have $200,000. Do I hear...

551
00:34:18,423 --> 00:34:20,892
Two hundred
thousand and one dollar.

552
00:34:21,226 --> 00:34:22,226
(CROWD LAUGHING)

553
00:34:25,230 --> 00:34:27,028
Mr. Wayne.

554
00:34:27,532 --> 00:34:29,558
How about you let someone
else have some fun?

555
00:34:29,834 --> 00:34:33,635
Oh! Sure. No problem.
I'm sorry about that.

556
00:34:38,476 --> 00:34:40,604
- Three hundred thousand.
- AUCTIONEER: Very well,

557
00:34:40,779 --> 00:34:41,872
three hundred thou...

558
00:34:41,947 --> 00:34:43,973
Three hundred thousand dollars

559
00:34:44,082 --> 00:34:45,380
and one cent.

560
00:34:45,917 --> 00:34:47,283
(CROWD LAUGHING)

561
00:34:47,385 --> 00:34:49,320
Five hundred thousand.

562
00:34:50,889 --> 00:34:52,653
Now back off. You little brat.

563
00:34:52,924 --> 00:34:55,758
Wow, Ms. Kean.
That's a lot of money.

564
00:34:56,394 --> 00:34:57,794
For someone like you.

565
00:34:57,996 --> 00:34:59,988
Two million dollars!

566
00:35:00,231 --> 00:35:01,426
(ALL GASPING)

567
00:35:01,900 --> 00:35:02,900
BARBARA: Two...

568
00:35:02,968 --> 00:35:05,096
Sold to Mr. Bruce Wayne.

569
00:35:10,575 --> 00:35:11,907
(LAUGHS)

570
00:35:14,479 --> 00:35:16,380
Congratulations, Bruce.

571
00:35:16,748 --> 00:35:18,148
Thank you.

572
00:35:21,119 --> 00:35:23,611
(LAUGHING) I do love
seeing that woman lose.

573
00:35:24,122 --> 00:35:25,750
A little piece of
friendly advice.

574
00:35:25,991 --> 00:35:27,823
Barbara Kean does
not give up easily.

575
00:35:27,926 --> 00:35:29,554
She'll be coming for that knife.

576
00:35:30,161 --> 00:35:32,926
I would just, uh, keep a
close eye on it if I were you.

577
00:35:37,669 --> 00:35:40,161
There's a new wrinkle, isn't it?

578
00:35:44,376 --> 00:35:46,345
What's green and red
and goes round and round?

579
00:35:47,012 --> 00:35:48,947
(BOTH CHUCKLE)

580
00:35:50,849 --> 00:35:52,818
Someone's obsessed
with the color red.

581
00:35:54,552 --> 00:35:56,646
The answer obviously
isn't newspaper.

582
00:35:57,455 --> 00:35:58,514
You're stalling.

583
00:35:58,957 --> 00:36:00,619
(CHUCKLES) No.

584
00:36:02,193 --> 00:36:03,320
You don't know the answer.

585
00:36:03,395 --> 00:36:04,488
Of course I do.

586
00:36:04,763 --> 00:36:05,992
No, you don't. Ed!

587
00:36:08,867 --> 00:36:10,699
- (GASPS)
- Mmm-hmm.

588
00:36:11,202 --> 00:36:14,001
Your brilliant mind is as
damaged as the rest of your body.

589
00:36:16,007 --> 00:36:17,031
Actually,

590
00:36:17,976 --> 00:36:19,205
my body is feeling fine.

591
00:36:22,480 --> 00:36:24,210
Frog in a blender!

592
00:36:33,558 --> 00:36:35,026
(WIND BLOWING)

593
00:36:39,197 --> 00:36:40,426
BRUCE: Selina?

594
00:36:43,501 --> 00:36:47,495
Why are you sitting
in the dark by yourself?

595
00:36:48,039 --> 00:36:49,337
I was expecting someone else.

596
00:36:49,407 --> 00:36:50,534
Like who?

597
00:36:50,608 --> 00:36:51,701
Doesn't matter.

598
00:36:51,776 --> 00:36:52,869
What are you doing here?

599
00:36:54,079 --> 00:36:56,514
I heard about Penguin's auction.

600
00:36:57,716 --> 00:36:59,685
So, Barbara sent
you for the knife.

601
00:37:00,251 --> 00:37:02,777
Figured, what? Because we're
friends I'd just hand it over?

602
00:37:03,121 --> 00:37:04,214
Something like that.

603
00:37:05,256 --> 00:37:06,519
Why is it so important to her?

604
00:37:06,591 --> 00:37:07,752
How should I know?

605
00:37:07,859 --> 00:37:10,055
She was supposed
to get it for some client.

606
00:37:10,662 --> 00:37:12,392
Said he was a
pretty dangerous guy.

607
00:37:13,598 --> 00:37:15,533
And that if you were smart,

608
00:37:15,667 --> 00:37:17,898
you'd hand it over and
save yourself the trouble.

609
00:37:18,837 --> 00:37:20,237
So, you're here to protect me.

610
00:37:20,338 --> 00:37:21,397
Is that it?

611
00:37:21,506 --> 00:37:23,668
The knife can't possibly
mean that much to you, Bruce.

612
00:37:23,742 --> 00:37:24,869
I don't know.

613
00:37:24,943 --> 00:37:27,879
Like you said, I spent $2
million on it. I think I'll keep it.

614
00:37:28,346 --> 00:37:30,212
- Why are you acting like this?
- Me?

615
00:37:31,349 --> 00:37:33,841
What about you? Are you really
Barbara Kean's errand girl now?

616
00:37:33,918 --> 00:37:35,386
I'm her partner.

617
00:37:36,020 --> 00:37:38,649
But if I don't give
her this knife back,

618
00:37:38,890 --> 00:37:41,052
she won't ever
see me as an equal.

619
00:37:44,395 --> 00:37:46,125
I'm asking you to do me a solid.

620
00:37:47,999 --> 00:37:49,331
I'm sorry, Selina.

621
00:37:50,001 --> 00:37:51,128
You should go.

622
00:38:17,395 --> 00:38:18,454
Morning, Harv.

623
00:38:19,164 --> 00:38:20,164
HARVEY: Jim, hey.

624
00:38:21,432 --> 00:38:24,561
This is Detective Harper. She just
transferred over from the three-five.

625
00:38:25,270 --> 00:38:26,990
- Jim Gordon.
- Nice to meet you, Detective.

626
00:38:27,071 --> 00:38:28,151
Heard a lot of good things.

627
00:38:28,239 --> 00:38:29,298
From who?

628
00:38:29,941 --> 00:38:31,581
It's good to have you
in the fold, Harper.

629
00:38:31,643 --> 00:38:32,643
Thanks.

630
00:38:36,915 --> 00:38:38,076
ALVAREZ: Gordon.

631
00:38:38,449 --> 00:38:39,747
You got a visitor.

632
00:38:41,219 --> 00:38:42,448
HARVEY: Hot damn.

633
00:38:43,321 --> 00:38:44,516
Fox in the hen house.

634
00:38:45,123 --> 00:38:46,182
No offense, Harper.

635
00:38:46,891 --> 00:38:48,018
HARPER: None taken.

636
00:38:49,694 --> 00:38:51,174
But you got the
wrong idiom, Captain.

637
00:38:51,229 --> 00:38:53,027
- No, I don't.
- Be right back.

638
00:38:57,202 --> 00:38:58,226
Sofia?

639
00:38:59,003 --> 00:39:00,027
What are you doing here?

640
00:39:00,705 --> 00:39:01,866
I came to help you.

641
00:39:02,574 --> 00:39:03,667
Yeah? How is that?

642
00:39:04,475 --> 00:39:08,003
Penguin. Oh, I know you
didn't directly ask me, but...

643
00:39:08,346 --> 00:39:10,679
I find men tend to have trouble
seeing the simple solution

644
00:39:10,748 --> 00:39:12,444
even when it's
presented to them.

645
00:39:12,517 --> 00:39:13,883
You shouldn't have
come back here.

646
00:39:15,153 --> 00:39:17,179
Oh, Jim.

647
00:39:18,056 --> 00:39:20,992
(EXHALES) I don't need
your permission. Or my father's.

648
00:39:21,793 --> 00:39:23,193
I'm a Falcone.

649
00:39:23,361 --> 00:39:24,886
This city is my birthright.

650
00:39:25,296 --> 00:39:26,736
My father might
not think I'm ready.

651
00:39:26,831 --> 00:39:28,629
But I am.

652
00:39:29,000 --> 00:39:30,195
Is that so?

653
00:39:31,970 --> 00:39:34,906
What do you think I've been doing
down south, the past 10 years?

654
00:39:39,744 --> 00:39:41,406
You knew you were coming back

655
00:39:41,512 --> 00:39:43,276
from the moment I
left your father's house.

656
00:39:44,082 --> 00:39:46,074
Everything that's
happened was what?

657
00:39:46,551 --> 00:39:47,883
You sizing me up?

658
00:39:47,952 --> 00:39:50,547
No. Not everything.

659
00:39:53,258 --> 00:39:55,591
But I had to get to know you
if we're gonna work together.

660
00:39:55,693 --> 00:39:56,888
We're not.

661
00:39:56,961 --> 00:39:59,954
Don't be sore, Jim. You
came looking for a gangster.

662
00:40:00,064 --> 00:40:01,589
That's exactly what you found.

663
00:40:03,935 --> 00:40:06,063
We can do great things
together, you and I.

664
00:40:07,205 --> 00:40:08,571
I'll be in touch.

665
00:40:15,413 --> 00:40:16,506
You?

666
00:40:17,282 --> 00:40:19,080
You're the one
who stole from me?

667
00:40:20,084 --> 00:40:21,416
MYRTLE: Some good it did.

668
00:40:21,853 --> 00:40:23,412
The ice broke his brain.

669
00:40:23,588 --> 00:40:24,783
What are you talking about?

670
00:40:25,690 --> 00:40:27,750
What's green and red
and goes round and round?

671
00:40:29,093 --> 00:40:30,186
Frog in a blender.

672
00:40:30,595 --> 00:40:32,894
Exactly. Ed couldn't
even get that.

673
00:40:34,632 --> 00:40:38,467
It does seem unlike Ed
to keep this rube alive.

674
00:40:39,737 --> 00:40:41,729
Sound the word.
I want him found.

675
00:40:43,041 --> 00:40:44,100
And her?

676
00:40:45,076 --> 00:40:47,568
A lot of people have been
trying to steal from me lately.

677
00:40:47,645 --> 00:40:49,238
An example needs to be made.

678
00:40:50,615 --> 00:40:52,447
Have fun, Victor.

679
00:41:01,492 --> 00:41:03,688
- Love your dress.
- Thank you.

680
00:41:04,095 --> 00:41:05,324
I made it. (SOBS)

681
00:41:23,848 --> 00:41:24,848
(EXHALES SHARPLY)

682
00:41:46,437 --> 00:41:47,437
(BOTH GRUNTING)

683
00:41:55,713 --> 00:41:57,682
Excellent technique, Barbara.

684
00:41:58,716 --> 00:41:59,911
I'm impressed.

685
00:42:00,752 --> 00:42:02,015
I had a good teacher.

686
00:42:03,521 --> 00:42:04,989
I wish you'd have called ahead.

687
00:42:09,193 --> 00:42:11,185
Does that mean you
don't have the knife?

688
00:42:12,330 --> 00:42:14,162
No. Bruce Wayne has it.

689
00:42:15,199 --> 00:42:16,360
Bruce?

690
00:42:17,268 --> 00:42:18,395
Really?

691
00:42:20,304 --> 00:42:21,533
How interesting.

692
00:42:23,641 --> 00:42:25,576
- You're not angry?
- No. Not at all.

693
00:42:25,676 --> 00:42:27,042
You see, Barbara,

694
00:42:27,145 --> 00:42:29,341
when I bathed you
in the Lazarus Pit,

695
00:42:29,814 --> 00:42:32,079
I did it in the knowledge
that you would become

696
00:42:32,350 --> 00:42:33,909
a competent ally of mine.

697
00:42:34,385 --> 00:42:35,580
And so far,

698
00:42:35,920 --> 00:42:37,582
you've done an admirable job.

699
00:42:41,025 --> 00:42:43,426
I'm glad you feel that way.

700
00:42:44,862 --> 00:42:46,694
And I wouldn't worry
about that knife.

701
00:42:48,266 --> 00:42:49,859
Didn't seem all
that special to me.

702
00:42:51,269 --> 00:42:53,602
Oh, but you couldn't
be more wrong.

703
00:42:54,205 --> 00:42:55,264
You see,

704
00:42:56,074 --> 00:42:57,235
that knife

705
00:42:58,042 --> 00:42:59,442
is the key

706
00:42:59,877 --> 00:43:01,470
to everything.

707
00:43:04,882 --> 00:43:06,612
Well, not everything.

708
00:43:53,598 --> 00:43:54,598
English - SDH
